{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1787273091328677001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"major-specified-bream","assignee":null,"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":10,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"b0329334c8e1f1219480ec2bb3c1a2aec57da540257917fd81fe28c212f8d289","event_uuid":"dc1ca79b-4a56-476b-982e-72b599587402","position":{"commit_sha":"b4fd506081d42f142aaf8c90feb57550005f7715","position":"1787270211033922001-dc1ca79b-4a56-476b-982e-72b599587402"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"8f26f8687ec1665877e98a164c0387ed8b0729aa1b5a7c09cd854cf349b7bd3d","event_uuid":"f94bbcfd-2c1e-4211-a62e-a612685347d2","position":{"commit_sha":"1806c0a0c0fa3819131fdee5462b97167dbbbcde","position":"1787270224939133001-f94bbcfd-2c1e-4211-a62e-a612685347d2"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"691dfe9e995e20806c7cba2d045de731ccc3a008ff0fc771c9a624d37bd25651","event_uuid":"d0e3f922-d6d3-4d05-a2b2-b308f73d71c6","position":{"commit_sha":"fc3d68494a74bb956f9ddbba5751b6a83d92352d","position":"1787270241104017001-d0e3f922-d6d3-4d05-a2b2-b308f73d71c6"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"02a7eadc050a8f7e9244b480456c69580dfb345d0b2c88eb0a588f914b373da9","event_uuid":"cb1ef2f8-1ed6-4a72-9b20-a334c0130319","position":{"commit_sha":"0d7b87a74d61b95f2b0e537c721fba93aee41839","position":"1787270249856034001-cb1ef2f8-1ed6-4a72-9b20-a334c0130319"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"9c3c647ae1744d1b20c5a8096fef0236e481937c4b2cb8974168deda1be27a93","event_uuid":"9c6b8fa5-b851-47d7-917a-4294bd2ee544","position":{"commit_sha":"173cd6da396fdf825e40116ef74a151b8e6fdf37","position":"1787270449867417001-9c6b8fa5-b851-47d7-917a-4294bd2ee544"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"5fe86c7bb73012fbd76eda8cc439249c13fa0b0fc6fe28140d17695cad03a809","event_uuid":"d6554573-e0ca-4fc1-8b2f-f066fe2fd506","position":{"commit_sha":"5a4c26e7c1692b25d9a4dd3694422feac26a43bd","position":"1787270455018369001-d6554573-e0ca-4fc1-8b2f-f066fe2fd506"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"f4e2b239e0649209d635101048a42067ec5f02b9234d321edd01c6e46b19c9df","event_uuid":"3c61dce1-0c95-458d-a5d0-a0aae9db6bd4","position":{"commit_sha":"fd8be238fcc67e4120c12964c0f4fc2d5f1bcf63","position":"1787270487566338001-3c61dce1-0c95-458d-a5d0-a0aae9db6bd4"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"54ecd92794d2043ab3086a948929d5e4f373db7e4d21d20e5f386f1106cc94ac","event_uuid":"e0e8b938-ead7-4b20-b454-9b1139c97bc7","position":{"commit_sha":"63a639c6226545c2a78dbbbbc63b775cb4f4ba94","position":"1787270914370721001-e0e8b938-ead7-4b20-b454-9b1139c97bc7"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"c117786ee5fa4dcd3fdf1d0bdd08d5a4d11e7ec5e8913193ca874e9d99abab41","event_uuid":"d5a248c0-2751-4f9e-b5c5-b043a0e6820c","position":{"commit_sha":"9f49cffcc0735e9a3e427bc8bbad482b8c8bb8a4","position":"1787270920105003001-d5a248c0-2751-4f9e-b5c5-b043a0e6820c"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"f82566d7619c76aee2f25bd22e27eb504a8663105a1031c2288c95be7c01fb0b","event_uuid":"7855c435-7690-46d9-b189-9ac5e39795c3","position":{"commit_sha":"322090d91a0ca29cb489372b7a6d2b4ff49dc65d","position":"1787270927869073001-7855c435-7690-46d9-b189-9ac5e39795c3"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## CORRECTION — the title's claim is NOT supported by the evidence I filed it on\n\nI filed this asserting blame auto-derivation \"is usually wrong\". **I conflated two different\nthings**, and the distinction destroys the claim:\n\n- **What I actually had:** each agent's analysis of what `git blame` on the *implicated lines*\n  would name. That is a hand-run blame over specific line ranges.\n- **What the ticket asserts:** that `caused_by` **auto-derivation** — a different mechanism, which\n  `docs/clone-guidance.md` describes as blaming *files* over the ticket's impacted paths — produces\n  wrong origins.\n\nI never observed the second. I inferred it from the first.\n\n## The direct evidence points the OTHER way\n\n`rebar transition` has **no `--caused-by` flag** (`brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee`,\n`0276-7eea-baf0-48e3`), and unknown flags are silently ignored. So on four closes today where the\nflag was passed per the `/rebar-debug` protocol, it did nothing and **the real auto-derivation ran**.\nIn every observed case it attached an edge pointing at **the same origin the agent had proven by\nhand**.\n\nThat is four out of four where the fallback agreed with a proven attribution. It is a small sample\nand the closes were not chosen to test this — but it is *direct* evidence about the actual\nmechanism, where my original table was *indirect* evidence about a different one.\n\n## What survives, and what does not\n\n**Does not survive:** the assertion that the fallback is usually wrong. Withdrawn. The title is\nwrong and should be read as the question it should have been.\n\n**Survives, and is still worth investigating:**\n\n- The **defect shapes** in the original table are real and were each proven: unmet obligations,\n  carrier commits, and omissions dominated this session's root causes. Those shapes are genuinely\n  hard to attribute, and hand-run `git blame` on the implicated lines misled in several of them.\n  That much stands on its own evidence.\n- Whether the file-level auto-derivation copes with those shapes **is now an open question with\n  evidence on both sides**, which is a better ticket than the one I filed.\n- The distinguishability question is untouched by the correction: a blame-derived edge and a\n  supplied one are indistinguishable on read, so the metric cannot weight them differently even if\n  someone measures the error rate.\n\n## Why this matters beyond tidying a ticket\n\nA ticket asserting a conclusion in its title shapes what an implementer looks for. Left as filed,\nthis one would have sent someone hunting for evidence that the fallback is broken — and there is a\nreal risk they would have found it, because the shapes ARE hard and a motivated search over 2,400\ntickets will surface examples. The measurement has to be able to come back \"the fallback is fine\".\n\nRetitling accordingly and rewriting the acceptance criteria so the ticket asks rather than asserts.\nThe `--caused-by` gap must be fixed FIRST, or the measurement is confounded: while the flag is\nsilently ignored, *every* edge is blame-derived regardless of what the closing agent believed, so\nthere is no \"supplied\" population to compare against.","timestamp":1787270449867417001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## Second correction — the counter-evidence in my retraction was itself wrong\n\nMy retraction argued the thesis was undermined because \"the flag never worked, so blame ran on all\nfour closes anyway — and landed on the same origin the agent had proven by hand.\"\n\n**The flag DOES work** (verified: `_parse_flags` at `_commands/transition.py:123-124,164`, the\npassing CLI interface test at `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_caused_by_autoderive.py:33`, and a\npeer's demonstration in a store with zero commits where blame could not have produced anything). So\non those closes the edges came from the **flag**, not from blame. Blame very likely never ran.\n\n**That specific counter-evidence is void.** I have no observations of the file-level auto-derivation\nagreeing with a proven origin, because I never observed it running.\n\n## What the ticket should say, now that both of my errors are subtracted\n\nThe **original retraction still holds, for its original reason**: I conflated each agent's\nhand-run `git blame` over *implicated lines* with the `caused_by` auto-derivation, which\n`docs/clone-guidance.md` describes as blaming *files* over impacted paths. Those are different\nmechanisms and I only ever observed the first. That conflation is what makes \"usually wrong\" an\nunsupported claim, and it is unaffected by the flag question.\n\nSo: the title should ask, not assert — which is how it now reads. But the evidence base is now\n**neither for nor against**. I have no direct data on the auto-derivation's accuracy in either\ndirection, and this ticket should say so plainly rather than carry my second wrong story.\n\nThe **defect shapes** in the original table remain proven and remain the reason the question is\nworth asking: unmet obligations, carrier commits, and omissions dominated this session's root\ncauses, and hand-run blame misled on several of them. Whether the file-level fallback copes with\nthose shapes is exactly what has not been measured.\n\nOne consequence for the measurement design: since `--caused-by` works, there **is** a distinguishable\n\"supplied\" population after all, so the `depends_on` link to `brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee` is no\nlonger a hard prerequisite — that ticket is now a P3 help-text fix, not a blocker. Reduced to\n`relates_to` unless a future reader disagrees.\n\nThree position changes on one question is more than this deserved. The durable lesson is the one\nthat caused all three: **I twice reasoned from an experiment that did not exercise the code path in\nquestion** — a stale working tree, then a no-op transition — and both times the error was caught by\nsomeone else re-deriving rather than by me.","timestamp":1787270914370721001}],"created_at":1787270211033922001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"d0e3f922-d6d3-4d05-a2b2-b308f73d71c6","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"10d0-c26c-bdbb-4b1f"},{"link_uuid":"cb1ef2f8-1ed6-4a72-9b20-a334c0130319","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"2399-5e49-6b28-4d59"},{"link_uuid":"7855c435-7690-46d9-b189-9ac5e39795c3","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"0276-7eea-baf0-48e3"}],"description":"## The observation\n\n`caused_by` auto-derivation falls back to best-effort `git blame` over a ticket's impacted paths\nwhen no `--caused-by` is supplied (`docs/clone-guidance.md`). Across **eight** attributions\nestablished under `/rebar-debug` in a single session (2026-08-20), each with a proven mechanism and\na deliberate blame check, that fallback would have produced a **wrong or empty** answer in roughly\nsix of eight cases.\n\nThis is not an argument that the fallback is badly implemented. It is an argument that the\n**dominant defect shape in this codebase is one blame cannot see**.\n\n## The evidence\n\n| bug | attributed origin | what blame would have named |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bediasite-bilineate-stoat` | `cec6-0a0e-38a9-4234` | correct — omission at origin, single commit |\n| `perkish-balsamic-pupfish` | `fc20-2df1-426e-42ab` | the GATE's author, whose code was correct when written. `fc20` extended administrative dispositions to task/story/epic, modified `close_precheck.py` and `transition_close.py`, and **never touched `gates.py`** |\n| `irongrey-chubby-oxpecker` | `789c-1251-fc21-4cf6` | a consumer written against a producer contract its own plan asserted but never verified |\n| `moist-short-lionfish` | `c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135` | `e1f4`, which WROTE `pending_enrichment` — correctly, for drain-time enumeration. `c1de` is the change that put it on the WRITE path under a 20 ms budget it declared itself |\n| `electronic-deducible-maggot` | `8d36-a15a-fcbd-4ebd` | a **June file-move carrier** in `test_cli.py` predating the transition entirely |\n| `steep-tired-whimbrel` | `c7c5-12ea-ad68-4690` | `051b82836bf`, a **carrier** (an extraction of identical logic); found by walking back with `git log -S \"could not read rebar config\"` |\n| `itchy-arbored-peccary` | `b73c-…` + `615c-…` (two) | nothing usable — both are **omissions**: a promise made in ADR 0005 D5 that no ticket's ACs ever obliged anyone to implement |\n| `ruling-magnific-bats` | `555e-3efa-4ad6-4169` | correct — here the cause site genuinely IS the fix site, established by the date test rather than assumed |\n| `nonodorous-snotty-seaurchin` | *unattributable* | the guard has been wrong since `tests/conftest.py`'s first commit, which predates the `rebar-ticket:` convention |\n\n## Why blame fails, stated precisely\n\nThree distinct mechanisms, all common here:\n\n1. **Unmet obligation.** A change creates a new contract — a field, a signature, an invariant, a\n   config key, a changed default — and discharges it on some consumers but not all. The consumer\n   that was missed may be untouched for months and still be where the failure surfaces and where\n   the fix lands. Blame names whoever last edited a line that was correct when written. This was\n   the single most common shape.\n2. **Carrier commits.** A reformat, rename, file move or mechanical extraction sits on top of the\n   real origin. `git blame -w -C -M` helps but does not always suffice; twice today the origin was\n   only reachable by `git log -S<symbol>` on the PRODUCER side.\n3. **Omission.** Nothing ever broke, so no commit made it wrong. An ADR promised a byte-total\n   backstop and no ticket's acceptance criteria ever obliged its implementation. There is no\n   commit for blame to find, by construction.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe `caused_by` edge exists to feed `rebar metrics`' escape-rate lenses and the epic-close\n`caused_by` floor. A **wrong** edge is worse than an absent one: it silently charges an escaped\ndefect to a ticket whose reviewers could not plausibly have caught it, and it is indistinguishable\nfrom a considered attribution on read. The fallback fails CLOSED on a sub-majority or a partial\nread, which is the right instinct — but the cases above are ones where blame returns a confident\nmajority for the wrong file.\n\nSo the metric's accuracy depends far more on agents supplying `--caused-by` explicitly than the\nexistence of a fallback suggests. That is worth knowing before anyone reads escape-rate numbers as\nif they were measured rather than largely inferred.\n\n## What this ticket should determine\n\n- **Measure it, do not take this sample as the answer.** Nine attributions from one session is a\n  suggestive sample, not a rate. Sample closed bugs carrying a `caused_by` edge, re-derive the\n  origin independently, and quantify how often the recorded edge is the blame-derived guess versus\n  a supplied proof — and how often the guess is wrong.\n- **Decide whether a wrong edge should be distinguishable from a proven one on read.** Today they\n  are not. A `derived: blame` versus `derived: explicit` marker on the edge would let the metric\n  weight them differently, or exclude guesses entirely. That is a small schema question with a\n  large effect on whether the numbers mean anything.\n- **Consider whether the fallback should run at all** when the mechanism is an omission or an\n  unmet obligation — cases it structurally cannot get right. It may be better to record nothing\n  and say so than to record a plausible wrong answer.\n- **Consider a review-time criterion** for the dominant shape: a change that introduces a new\n  contract should be asked whether every consumer of that contract was updated. That is the\n  producer-side question, and it is the one nobody is currently asked.\n\n## Relationship to sibling tickets\n\n- `marshy-chummy-coot` (`2399-5e49-6b28-4d59`) asks how a silently-inert guard escaped review and\n  tests. This ticket asks a different question — not how a defect escaped DETECTION, but why the\n  record of where it CAME FROM is unreliable. Related, not duplicate.\n- `ruling-magnific-bats` (`10d0-c26c-bdbb-4b1f`) is the mechanical defect in the same area: the\n  close's `--caused-by` write is not idempotent. Its second comment argues the guidance to always\n  pass the flag must stand precisely BECAUSE the blame fallback is unreliable — which is the claim\n  this ticket exists to test rather than assume.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The rate is measured, not estimated: a sample of closed bugs with `caused_by` edges is\n      re-derived independently, and the proportion of blame-derived edges that name a defensibly\n      wrong origin is reported with the sample size and method.\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether a blame-derived edge should be distinguishable from an\n      explicitly supplied one on read, with the reasoning.\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether the fallback should decline to attribute for the shapes it\n      cannot get right, rather than guessing.\n- [ ] Any proposed review-time criterion or schema change is filed as its own ticket rather than\n      implemented here.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"none","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[["depends_on","0276-7eea-baf0-48e3"],["relates_to","0276-7eea-baf0-48e3"],["relates_to","10d0-c26c-bdbb-4b1f"],["relates_to","2399-5e49-6b28-4d59"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"Measurement and decision ticket: samples existing closed bugs, re-derives origins, and records decisions. Any resulting schema change or review criterion is filed as its own ticket rather than implemented here.","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"50e3-6d2a-0e7f-4e92","ticket_type":"story","title":"Measure whether caused_by blame auto-derivation is reliable for this codebase's dominant defect shapes","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["dc1ca79b-4a56-476b-982e-72b599587402","f94bbcfd-2c1e-4211-a62e-a612685347d2","d0e3f922-d6d3-4d05-a2b2-b308f73d71c6","cb1ef2f8-1ed6-4a72-9b20-a334c0130319","9c6b8fa5-b851-47d7-917a-4294bd2ee544","d6554573-e0ca-4fc1-8b2f-f066fe2fd506","3c61dce1-0c95-458d-a5d0-a0aae9db6bd4","e0e8b938-ead7-4b20-b454-9b1139c97bc7","d5a248c0-2751-4f9e-b5c5-b043a0e6820c","7855c435-7690-46d9-b189-9ac5e39795c3"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787273091328677001,"uuid":"ec9baa93-7b54-4d36-881a-8511a2b4c542"}